this is a production of cornell  university  okay so we've been talking about the  physical aspects of soil  but there's a chemical aspect of soil  that we can test very easily  that is ph which is measures the acidity  or  alkalinity of any substance and in soils  ph determines very much what  nutrients are available to plants so  some plants  can tolerate a wide range of ph and some  have a very narrow range of ph where  they grow well in so we need to know  this in terms of plant selection  the ph test is very simple we use a  cornell ph  kit which is a color metric test  and we'll take we'll try two different  soils there's a  two parts of the kit one is the standard  and one is a wide range we always start  with the standard  so we have these cups where we're going  to put a little bit of soil in one of  the depressions  i'm not going to fill it up just a  little bit of soil  and then we're going to take one of the  reagents here  there are two we always start with  bromothymol blue  which is the neutral range we want to  start with the neutral range and then we  can go up  more alkaline or go down more acid  so using brown thymol blue we're going  to drop drop by drop  until we have a little bit of free  solution  we're going to mix this oh yes  for about 10 to 20 seconds  so that the reagent mixes with the soil  and then we're going to allow that  solution to go down the channel  in this case there's quite a bit of soil  there but i can still see a color  what we're looking for is the color in  the channel  so we use a color chart to determine  what  that ph is saying uh there's reading  and we in this chart it says brom final  blue on the top  so we're going to look at the color we  find  in the channel versus the color chart  i'd say somewhere between 6.0 and 6.2  based on the color of the chart maybe  6.0  okay so if we're at one end of the color  chart we want to go  further because we may not see exactly  what  the final ph would be so in this case  we'll use  another reagent in the kit which goes  from ph  5 to 6.2 it's at the lower end so we  take another bit of soil from our  sample put it in the other bowl  and using chlorophenol red  we're going to wet the sample  and mix it by tapping it  so that the solution bathes the soil in  the  cup  now we're going to allow that  bead to go down the channel  back and forth and we're going to use  the  color chart at the bottom which says  chlorophenyl red  to see what our  color is you can see  we're at the six  to six point two range so in fact the  six point two which we  saw with brampton blue is correct  because we've uh corroborated that with  another  solution so here is six six point two  pretty close so i'd say it's about  six to six point two ph and that would  be the final  determination for this soil now when  we're changing soils and we're going to  do another soil or another sample we  always want to clean our plate with  deionized water so we're not going to  change  use the ph of the tap water to change  our  our reading  so i have another soil here which you  want to test  you can't tell anything about the soil  by looking at it in terms of what the ph  is and that determines  very importantly how much certain  elements are available to  plant roots so we're going to put a  little bit of  soil in the bowl start again with  bromothymol blue  which is the neutral range drop drop  drop  do we have a little bit of free  solution  mixing it with the soil and then getting  a bead down the channel  and here instead of that kind of  yellowish green we have almost a blue  green  color which would be  at the end of the bronzer blue chart  sort of 7.2 but again being that we're  at the end we need to go further  to see if that's the final range of ph  so here we take the wide range test kit  which has goes higher  with phenol red or much lower with brom  crease or green  we're going to go higher with phenol red  because we start stopped at 7.2 we want  to go  up in terms of the seeing what the real  ph  is so here we're going to put some more  soil in the second bowl  by the way you want to have your hands  clean when you do this  so you're not introducing anything into  the sample  so here we're going to take phenol red  which is the wide high range  to see what the soil actually is  and we're going to mix it sometimes you  see the color change when you're mixing  it  and then we're gonna get a bead down the  channel  and what we're looking for is the film  the color film  in this case we use the color chart for  phenol red  which is pretty bright  and here we're seeing we're not up at  seven we're  more toward i'd say  it's quite blue so we're probably at 8.4  or 8.6  so we have a much more alkaline soil  here than we did  in the first sample and that's going to  determine  which plants can grow well in some of  those types of soils  so we need to know that for plant  selection and it's a very simple test  that you can do without any special  equipment  this has been a production of cornell  university  on the web at cornell.edu
 

